\enlargethispage{6\baselineskip}
\vspace{-8pt}
\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}
\vspace{-4pt}

\looseness=-2 We showed that learned post-hoc correction of frozen flow-matching VLAs is harmful by default. Under a K-matched design ($K{=}2$, same $\varepsilon$ and CRM checkpoint), unconditional correction degrades all four LIBERO suites on SmolVLA (0.4B, up to $-$14.8\,pp on Long) and all three evaluated suites on $\pi_{0.5}$ (3.6B, up to $-$14.0\,pp on Goal), with degradation amplified $3.8{\times}$ on stronger models. The root cause is that 80--97\% of control steps are already confident; corrections on these steps inject noise rather than signal. Conformal confidence sets constructed from $K{=}2$ pairwise $L_2$ distances ($\rho{=}0.9979$) detect these confident steps and gate the CRM via a calibrated threshold $\tau$. This $\tau$-gating recovers all degradation ($\Delta_{\mathrm{gate}}{=}{+}2.8$ to $+$14.5\,pp), turning a universally harmful module into a selective refinement tool. The key finding is that \emph{knowing when not to correct} matters more than correction strength.

\vspace{-4pt}
\section{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}
\vspace{-2pt}

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Effect sizes and statistical power.}\enspace Paired improvements ($\tau$-gated vs.\ VLA-only) reach $p{=}0.017$ on Spatial ($n{=}8$; Appendix~\ref{sec:8seed}) but do not reach significance on other suites with $n{=}3$. The primary claim is $\tau$-gated vs.\ CRM always-on under the K-matched design: conformal gating exceeds unconditional correction on all four suites ($\Delta_{\mathrm{gate}}{=}{+}2.8$ to $+$14.5\,pp), with Long providing the clearest signal ($\Delta_{\mathrm{gate}}{=}{+}14.5$\,pp).

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Confidently wrong steps.}\enspace The pairwise $L_2$ probe measures sample disagreement, not correctness. On Long (trigger rate ${\sim}$3\%), the VLA may produce confident but systematically incorrect outputs that escape gating.

\looseness=-1 \textbf{Scope and generalization.}\enspace The CRM requires task-specific calibration data (10 demos per task); deterministic VLAs lie outside scope (Appendix~\ref{sec:applicability}). The threshold $\tau$ controls a precision--recall tradeoff: optimal $\tau$ is suite-dependent (Appendix~\ref{sec:tau_sweep}), motivating adaptive $\tau$ selection as future work. All experiments use LIBERO simulation; real-robot transfer remains future work. The $\pi_{0.5}$ analysis (Section~\ref{sec:cross_vla}) reveals that correction harm amplifies as baseline capability increases; broader cross-architecture validation is needed.

